REGRESSION TEST

If your system is configured and you compiled everything
just try the tests with
> make

To do many tests in parallel, as usual
> make -j 4

Tests that request `mpiprocs` prefer a directly compiled native-MPI kernel
and its configured MPI launcher. Otherwise a kernel with embedded thread-MPI
uses the internal launcher for PLUMED command-line tools, including driver and
`type=plumed` tests such as benchmark, and make tests using the shared MPI
harness described below. Other external MPI executables require a native-MPI
build. Existing per-test MPI settings remain unchanged,
so old PLUMED versions continue using their native launcher. An explicit
`PLUMED_MPIRUN` overrides internal thread-MPI launching.

For a second pass through an MPI-injecting host, set
`PLUMED_CLI_PROGRAM_NAME=/absolute/path/to/mpi-host` and `PLUMED_MPIRUN`.
The runner determines the installed kernel/library paths; `PLUMED_KERNEL` and
`PLUMED_LIB` can override those paths. The CLI override applies only to CLTool
cases; external-engine cases retain `PLUMED_PROGRAM_NAME` (or `plumed`) and
their existing native-MPI capability requirements. The host initializes and
finalizes real MPI for calculations, while honoring `--no-mpi` for queries.
No provider environment variable or kernel rebuild is needed.

The ordinary serial executable does not initialize a dynamically loaded real
MPI runtime. Leave `PLUMED_MPI_WRAPPER` unset for normal internal-thread-MPI
suite runs. Host injection is tested separately by
`regtest/mpiwrap/check-mpi-host-injection.sh`: an MPI-compiled host initializes
MPI, creates an ordinary object, injects with `injectMPIImplementation`, runs
representative input, destroys its PLUMED object, and finalizes MPI.
See `src/mpiwrap/README.md`
for the complete direct-MPI and host-injection validation matrix.

A test that cannot run with internal threads (for example, one that depends on
an unsupported working-directory change) can set `plumed_thread_mpi=no` in its
`config`. Such configs can set `plumed_chdir=yes` as well to report the specific
unsupported-CHDIR skip reason. Launching a tool with multiple thread-MPI ranks
does not itself add MPI parallelization to that tool; simplemd is currently
not MPI-parallelized.

SHARED MPI HARNESS FOR MAKE TESTS

A make test can use the existing native/thread-MPI rank launcher through
`regtest/scripts/mpi-test.h`. Set `plumed_mpi_harness=yes` in its config and
include `../../scripts/mpi-test.h` in `extra_files`. The executable calls
`PLMD::mpi_test::run(argc,argv,expected_ranks,body)`, where body has signature
`int(MPI_Comm,int,char**)`. Construct all mutable objects inside body and use
its communicator instead of MPI_COMM_WORLD. The helper handles startup and
finalization and consumes the runner's `-n N` argument; other arguments reach
body unchanged. The existing `mpiprocs` settings stay intact. Older native-MPI
runners without `-n` and older PLUMED builds without mpiwrap remain supported.

Keep conversions mechanical: preserve the calculations, file ownership, and
references. The empty-rank, first-step-inactive, and nonfinite-trace MPI tests
currently remain native-only because they change process-wide environment
variables. `tools/rt-Neigbourlist-omp` also remains native-only: its MPI rank
setting currently starts independent serial copies, rather than an MPI body.
These cases need a separate design decision before using internal threads.

HOW TO ADD A NEW TEST

Add a new directory rtXXX, let's say rt105, and go there:
> cd rt105

Put a standard makefile there:
> echo "include ../scripts/test.make" > rt105/Makefile

Put in the rt105 directory a "config" files containing the
options which define your test.
Presently, you can try one of the following:

(A) testing with simplemd
type="simplemd"

(B) testing with the driver. the arg variable allows to pass command
    line arguments to the driver
type="driver"
arg="--plumed=plumed.dat --timestep=0.05 trajectory.xyz"

Put in the rt105 directory all the input files that you need.
Typically:
  for simplemd: in input.xyz plumed.dat
  for driver: trajectory.xyz plumed.dat
Many tests share can share the same trajectory file. Just add
to the config file a line similar to
extra_files="../trajectories/name-of-the-file"
to include a common trajectory file

Make:
> make

Copy from the newly generated tmp directory the files that you
want to check and rename them into xxx.reference
> cp rt5/COLVAR COLVAR.reference

Add to the git repository all the files in the rt105 directory,
but for report.txt and tmp directory:
> git add .
(this should work since report.txt and tmp are git-ignored)
In this manner, other people will be able to perform the test

HOW TO RESET A TEST
If you get an error from a test but you think that the new
result is the correct one, just go to its directory and type
> make reset
Then commit the modified references


